      "activity": "Group Discussion: Why does the open cover {(1/n, 1)} fail to have a finite subcover for (0,1)? Have students draw diagrams representing the 'shrinking' nature of the cover near 0."
    },
    {
      "phase": "Core Theory & Proof Workshop (30 mins)",
      "activity": "Guided Proof: Split class into small groups. Assign each group one direction of the equivalence proof (Compact \u2192 Sequentially Compact OR Sequentially Compact \u2192 Complete & Totally Bounded). Groups present their logic to the class."
    },
    {
      "phase": "Counter-Example Hunt (15 mins)",
      "activity": "Challenge: Provide examples of spaces that are (a) Bounded but not Compact, (b) Complete but not Compact. Students must identify the missing property (e.g., l\u00b2 space unit ball)."
    },
    {
      "phase": "Summary & Q&A (10 mins)",
      "activity": "Recap the hierarchy of properties (Compact \u2192 Complete \u2192 Cauchy Sequences Converge). Address remaining questions."
    }
  ],
  "assessment_methods": [
    "Formative: Observation of group discussions and proof workshop participation.",
    "Summative: Short quiz requiring identification of compact sets in various metric spaces.",
    "Homework: Prove that a continuous function on a compact metric space is uniformly continuous (Cantor's Theorem)."
  ],
  "recommended_resources": [
    "Munkres, J. R. 'Topology', Chapter 4 (Compactness).",
    "Rudin, W. 'Principles of Mathematical Analysis', Chapter 2 (Basic Topology).",
    "Willard, S. 'General Topology', Section on Metric Spaces.",
    "Visual aids: Interactive topology software (e.g., GeoGebra for visualizing covers in \u211d\u00b2)."
  ]
}

