        "type": "Interactive Experiment",
        "description": "Ask students to choose between Option A (Guaranteed $50) and Option B (50% chance of $100, 50% chance of $0). Then ask them to choose between Option C (Guaranteed loss of $50) and Option D (50% chance of losing $100, 50% chance of losing nothing).",
        "learning_goal": "Demonstrate Risk Aversion in gains and Risk Seeking in losses (Reflection Effect)."
      },
      "activity_2": {
        "name": "The Asian Disease Problem (Framing)",
        "type": "Group Discussion",
        "description": "Present the classic Tversky & Kahneman scenario where 600 people are at risk. Group 1 sees '200 saved' vs '1/3 chance all saved'. Group 2 sees '400 die' vs '1/3 chance none die'. Compare results.",
        "learning_goal": "Illustrate how identical outcomes framed as lives saved vs. lives lost alter decision-making."
      },
      "activity_3": {
        "name": "Real-World Case Analysis",
        "type": "Case Study",
        "description": "Analyze insurance purchasing behavior or lottery ticket buying through the lens of Probability Weighting and Loss Aversion.",
        "learning_goal": "Connect theory to everyday consumer behavior."
      }
    },
    "assessment_methods": [
