    "key_points": [
      "Definition: Consideration is the price paid for a promise; it involves a benefit to the promisor or a detriment to the promisee.",
      "Sufficiency vs. Adequacy: Consideration must have some economic value (sufficient) but courts do not assess fairness (adequacy).",
      "Timing: Consideration must be present or future; past consideration is generally not valid.",
      "Existing Duty: Performing an existing public or contractual duty usually lacks fresh consideration.",
      "Modern Exception: Practical benefit may validate consideration in contract modifications (Williams v Roffey Bros)."
    ],
