      "The Principle of Proportionality: Prohibiting attacks where expected civilian harm is excessive compared to the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated.",
      "The Principle of Military Necessity: Justifying only those measures which are indispensable for securing the complete submission of the enemy.",
      "The Principle of Humanity (Prohibition of Unnecessary Suffering): Forbidding weapons or methods of warfare that cause superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering.",
      "The Principle of Precaution: Taking all feasible precautions to minimize harm to civilians and civilian objects during attacks."
    ],
    "difficulties": [
      {
        "concept": "Application of Proportionality",
        "challenge": "Students often struggle with the subjective nature of weighing 'military advantage' against 'civilian harm' in real-time decision-making."
      },
      {
        "concept": "Combatant Status in Asymmetric Warfare",
        "challenge": "Distinguishing between combatants and civilians becomes difficult in non-international armed conflicts involving non-state actors."
      },
      {
        "concept": "Humanity vs. Necessity",
        "challenge": "Understanding the tension between achieving military goals quickly and adhering to humanitarian constraints."
      }
    ],
    "classroom_activity_design": [
      {
        "activity_name": "Case Study Analysis: The Targeted Strike",
        "type": "Group Discussion",
        "description": "Divide students into groups. Provide a redacted scenario of a drone strike targeting a high-value individual in a populated area. Groups must analyze the operation using the principles of Distinction, Proportionality, and Precaution.",
        "time_allocation": "30 Minutes",
        "output": "A brief written assessment determining if the strike was lawful under IHL."
      },
      {
        "activity_name": "Role-Play: The Rules of Engagement Meeting",
        "type": "Simulation",
        "description": "Assign roles: Military Commander, Legal Advisor (JAG), Humanitarian Officer. Simulate a briefing before a mission. The Commander wants speed, the Humanitarian Officer wants protection, the Legal Advisor must enforce IHL principles.",
        "time_allocation": "25 Minutes",
        "output": "A consensus set of Rules of Engagement (ROE) drafted by the group."
      },
      {
        "activity_name": "Quick Fire Quiz",
        "type": "Individual Assessment",
        "description": "10-question multiple-choice quiz testing definitions and basic application of the five principles.",
        "time_allocation": "10 Minutes",
        "output": "Quiz score."
      }
    ],
    "assessment_methods": [
      "Participation in Group Discussions (20%)",
      "Role-Play Performance and ROE Drafting (30%)",
