{
  "topic": "One-Dimensional Quantum Harmonic Oscillator",
  "objective": "Derive energy eigenvalues and explain the origin of quantization through wave function boundary conditions.",
  "derivation_steps": [
    {
      "step": 1,
      "title": "Setup the Schrödinger Equation",
      "description": "Start with the time-independent Schrödinger equation: H psi = E psi, where the Hamiltonian H includes kinetic energy and the potential V(x) = (1/2)m omega^2 x^2."
    },
    {
      "step": 2,
      "title": "Introduce Dimensionless Variables",
      "description": "Define a dimensionless variable xi = sqrt(m omega / hbar) * x and a parameter lambda = 2E / (hbar omega) to simplify the differential equation."
    },
    {
      "step": 3,
      "title": "Analyze Asymptotic Behavior",
      "description": "Examine the behavior of the wave function as |x| approaches infinity. Show that psi(xi) must decay exponentially as exp(-xi^2 / 2) to remain physically valid."
    },
    {
      "step": 4,
      "title": "Series Solution Ansatz",
      "description": "Propose a solution of the form psi(xi) = h(xi) * exp(-xi^2 / 2), where h(xi) is a power series sum(a_j * xi^j)."
    },
    {
      "step": 5,
      "title": "Recurrence Relation",
      "description": "Substitute the ansatz into the differential equation to derive a recurrence relation for the coefficients a_j of the power series."
    },
    {
      "step": 6,
      "title": "Apply Boundary Conditions",
      "description": "Argue that for the wave function to be normalizable (square-integrable), the power series h(xi) must terminate. If it does not terminate, it grows faster than the exponential decay term, violating the boundary condition at infinity."
    },
    {
      "step": 7,
      "title": "Quantization Condition",
      "description": "Show that the series terminates only if lambda equals an odd integer (2n + 1), which imposes the condition n = 0, 1, 2, ..."
    },
    {
      "step": 8,
      "title": "Final Energy Expression",
      "description": "Substitute lambda back into the energy definition to obtain the quantized energy levels: E_n = hbar omega (n + 1/2)."
    }
  ],
  "discussion_points": [
    "The requirement that the wave function vanishes at infinity (normalizability) is the physical constraint that forces the mathematical series to terminate.",
    "This termination condition restricts the allowed energy values to discrete levels, demonstrating how boundary conditions lead to energy quantization.",
    "The ground state energy is non-zero (zero-point energy), a direct consequence of the uncertainty principle inherent in the Gaussian ground state wave function."
  ],
  "key_equations": [
    "-hbar^2/(2m) d^2psi/dx^2 + (1/2)m omega^2 x^2 psi = E psi",
    "d^2psi/dxi^2 + (lambda - xi^2)psi = 0",
    "E_n = hbar omega (n + 1/2)"
  ]
}

