      "action": "Clearly define the three guarantees.",
      "details": [
        "Consistency (C): Every read receives the most recent write or an error.",
        "Availability (A): Every request receives a (non-error) response, without the guarantee that it contains the most recent write.",
        "Partition Tolerance (P): The system continues to operate despite an arbitrary number of messages being dropped or delayed by the network."
      ]
    },
    "step_2_explain_the_trade_off": {
      "action": "Elucidate why C, A, and P cannot coexist during a failure.",
      "logic": "In a distributed system, network partitions (P) are inevitable. When a partition occurs, nodes cannot communicate. To maintain Consistency (C), the system must block requests until communication is restored (sacrificing A). To maintain Availability (A), the system must respond to requests using local data, which may be stale (sacrificing C)."
    },
